Impetigo
Paediatric & Neonatal
Honey-colored crusted lesions, Perioral/perinasal distribution, Painless pruritic vesicles

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-025
Reason for Visit: Rapidly spreading, crusted facial skin lesions causing severe caregiver concern for aggressive bacterial infection or herpes zoster.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a rapidly expanding, weeping rash on a child's face that appeared overnight. Immediate after-hours assessment was mandated to rule out severe periorbital cellulitis, staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, and to initiate targeted antimicrobial therapy to halt the highly contagious spread prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He had a tiny scratch on his nose yesterday, and today his whole face is covered in these weeping, crusty yellow sores."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with honey-colored, heavily crusted lesions characteristically clustered around the mouth and nose. The mother reports the initial presentation was a few fragile, thin-walled vesicles that rapidly ruptured. The superficial skin lesions are painless but highly pruritic, provoking repeated scratching. Autoinoculation is evident, spreading the lesions to the child's chin and left cheek via contaminated fingers. The patient lacks any systemic toxicity, fever, or deep tissue pain. The mother notes a sibling had a similar, smaller lesion last week.
Functional Status: Active, eating well, constantly touching the face.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of contagious transmission to siblings and the wider daycare cohort.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 22 Kg
General: Well-appearing, alert, afebrile, in no acute distress.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals multiple superficial, honey-colored, crusted plaques located in a perioral and perinasal distribution. When the thick superficial crust is gently moved, a visibly erythematous, weeping base remains. There is localized, mild submandibular regional lymphadenopathy directly draining the affected cutaneous areas. The periorbital tissue is entirely soft, non-tender, and without erythema. The diagnostic tetrad of honey-colored crusted lesions, perioral/perinasal distribution, and painless pruritic vesicles is definitively confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Multiple children sharing a small play space.
Medication Reconciliation: Over-the-counter antiseptic cream applied with no improvement.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Impetigo (Non-bullous) - Status: Active, spreading infection
Differentials:

Herpes Simplex Virus (Cold Sores) - Ruled out by the diffuse, expanding honey-colored crusting rather than a strictly localized cluster of extremely painful vesicles on an erythematous base at the vermilion border.

Periorbital Cellulitis - Ruled out explicitly by the strictly superficial nature of the lesions, complete lack of deep tissue swelling, normal eye movements, and afebrile status.

Atopic Dermatitis - Ruled out by the sudden, acute onset of purulent crusting, though impetigo often secondarily infects existing eczematous lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk), Moderate (Infectious transmission risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The rapid overnight spread of facial lesions in a young child necessitated an urgent after-hours clinical assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the critical need to exclude deep tissue infections, specifically periorbital cellulitis, which threatens vision and the central nervous system. Objective clinical evaluation confirmed the classic superficial, honey-crusted lesions of non-bullous impetigo, completely ruling out deep fascial or periorbital involvement. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in continued aggressive autoinoculation, prolonged discomfort, and widespread transmission within the household. Immediate intervention provided diagnostic clarity and allowed for the prompt initiation of topical and systemic antimicrobial therapy to rapidly sterilize the lesions.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Mupirocin 2% ointment to be applied TDS to the lesions. Given the multiple sites and rapid spread, also prescribed oral Cephalexin 25mg/kg/dose BD for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: Require the child to immediately wash their hands with a brightly colored, preferred foaming soap every time they are observed touching their face. By making the required handwashing an immediate, slightly inconvenient, but sensory-pleasing consequence, the child is actively conditioned to stop touching the highly pruritic lesions, breaking the cycle of autoinoculation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to confirm the lesions are drying out and not expanding further.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the redness rapidly spreads deep into the skin, if the child's eyelid becomes swollen shut, if they develop a high fever, or if the skin becomes exquisitely painful to touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Impetigo is highly contagious until 24 hours of appropriate antibiotic therapy has been completed.
Clinical Course: The sores will stop oozing and dry up within 48 hours of starting antibiotics. The crusts will eventually fall off without leaving permanent scars.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the skin around the eye becomes red and swollen, or if the child develops a high temperature.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is a superficial bacterial infection of the top layer of the skin. The topical ointment directly kills the bacteria on the surface, while the oral antibiotic stops the infection from spreading internally.
Actionable Steps: Gently wash the crusts away with warm water and soap before applying the antibiotic ointment. Wash all the child's towels and bed sheets in hot water tonight, and strictly do not share towels.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Impetigo (School Sores). No deep tissue infection. Start topical and oral antibiotics. Keep home from daycare for 24h.

SMS EDUCATION

Wash child's hands immediately if they touch their face. Wash all towels in hot water. Go to ED if eyelid swells shut.

